AI Clinical Trial Card: The Uneven Impact of Generative AI on Entrepreneurial Performance

Authors: Alex Otis, Rembrand Koning, Sampsa Samila, and colleagues

AI Model Details

Provider: OpenAI
Model Name and Version: GPT-4 (exact version not specified)

Access Method:

Chat-based interface provided to participants as part of the experimental platform

Fine-tuning:

No fine-tuning reported

Model used in a general-purpose configuration

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Participants were given structured prompts to guide interaction with GPT-4

No explicit reporting of temperature or decoding parameters

Performance on Standard Benchmarks:

Not reported

Human Participant Profile

Sample Size:

Approximately 300+ participants (exact counts vary by task and condition)

Population / Expertise:

Entrepreneurs and startup founders

Participants exhibited heterogeneous levels of experience and skill

Domain Expertise:

Business ideation, venture strategy, and entrepreneurial decision-making

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Likely low-to-moderate

Study conducted during early diffusion of GPT-4 into professional settings

Training / Onboarding:

Participants received guidance on how to interact with the AI system

Emphasis on using AI as a support tool rather than a replacement

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Entrepreneurial tasks such as:

Generating business ideas

Refining and improving venture concepts

Interface:

Chat-based interaction with GPT-4

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive

AI provided suggestions, feedback, and alternative ideas; final decisions remained with participants

Experimental Conditions:

Control (no AI assistance)

GPT-4 assistance

Primary Outcome Measures:

Expert-rated quality of entrepreneurial ideas

Evaluations conducted by domain experts

Main Effects:

On average, access to GPT-4 improved entrepreneurial performance

However, effects were highly uneven:

Lower-performing participants benefited substantially

High-performing participants saw smaller gains or, in some cases, performance declines

Variance in outcomes increased under AI assistance

Notes on Interpretation

This paper is central for understanding distributional effects of generative AI

Rather than uniformly raising performance, AI reshapes the performance distribution

Results suggest that generative AI may act as a leveling technology in some contexts while potentially dampening differentiation among top performers

Highlights the importance of examining who benefits, not just average treatment effects, in human–AI experiments